 button does nothing in the code tab.. aloso the ui for clearglass is cut off.": the Code tab's agent button showed on the changes view, which never drew the panel, and a picked line held the panel open on files — the panel now sits beside the changes and a press always shows or hides it (test-code-tab CT-11b, Clear Glass); a closed Co-pilot kept its 340 px because the 0.60.180 shell rule outranked .hidden — the black strip down the right of every page (test-browser-shell BST-09b at 1920 px: 340 before, 0 after). See CHANGELOG-0.60.206.md.] [0.60.205: James, with his 0.60.201 run log: "show me idearium improvements that would help the most from the phases" · "the log fixes also. start them". SD21, six fixes read from the log: the background probe asks whether its model fits free memory before loading it (its own 7b load drove memory critical every ten minutes); a build's job behind a sign-in wall is sent back so the ladder climbs (claude held one 295 s), a person's still waits; the shadow's absent line says file / event / condition (an unmet condition read like a missing file — the coder first misread it too); a proof run's per-run history stays out of the repo's store (35 → 65 spec chunks in an evening); a phase that asked for one file answers the "which file?" question itself; the Nexus sync pauses between systems when memory goes. test-log-fixes 6/6, test-proof-run 7/7. Not yet: the signed-out claude tab reconnecting every ~9 s. See CHANGELOG-0.60.205.md.] [0.60.204: James: "also fix chatgpt. i can't login. i click login and it pops up for a second and dissapears". "Continue with Google" opens a popup that takes focus; the ChatGPT window behind it was visible but unfocused, which the attention handler read as the hidden agent tab, so it sent ChatGPT (works signed out) back to its chat and the popup, its opener gone, closed. A window with its own sign-in popup open is now his: not sent back, not focused, not hidden mid-sign-in (clear-glass/src/providers/attention.js 1.4.0; login.live.com, auth0.openai.com and Cloudflare's challenge count as sign-in pages). test-cg-attention-focus 7/7 (AF-02c). See CHANGELOG-0.60.204.md.] [0.60.203: James, after his SP5 run at 1–9% free memory, to the coder's three fixes (stop the officiator's loop; a pinned local model that won't fit goes to the browser agents and says why; a build step never auto-applies a file it wasn't asked for): "yes". SD20. (1) The officiator turned each of the build's replies into the same goal ("Build phase SP5_title by ensuring lib/title.js…") about every 30 s, each contract blocked or failed: a goal already queued, running or failed within the hour is now marked on the drop (duplicateOf), not queued again; after 3 of its own contracts fail in a row it stops synthesizing for an hour (unpark all clears it); memory is read before each synthesis, and the RAID worker waits on memory too. (2) Settings pinning Ollama with no ladder now climbs the pin, then the chain's agents: a model that is missing or won't fit free memory is left off, critical memory moves it last, said on the route. (3) While a phase builds, an agent's file outside the phase's files — or a spec/doc while it asked for code — is held as a proposal with why, whatever the inject mode (repo-inject land and code-edit commit). test-starved-machine 5/5, test-raid-officiator 18/18. See CHANGELOG-0.60.203.md.] [0.60.202: James, after SP5's title.js was refused again and again: "need a retry or automatically ask for the fix if possible, or a tool or something" · "still didn't work." · "that error needs to be fixed." The gate refused each Gemini attempt because it changed the error message tests/sp5-title.test.js checks, and Gemini never saw which test or what it expected. SD19: an agent's refused proposal now goes back to its agent the way his Reject does — rejected with the failing test, expected and actual, as the reason; told; the phase built again within the daily cap — and the answer says so (repos.retry_on_refusal:false leaves it to him; a person's change is untouched). Toasts: one identical message counted ×N instead of five stacked boxes; a long one shows its first line, the whole on hover. test-refusal-sendback 4/4 (RS-03 in Clear Glass). See CHANGELOG-0.60.202.md.] [0.60.201: James, with his upload dialog showing "could not start: signal is aborted without reason" and his boot log: "okay, remember this is for any project. not just mmine". The dialog's first step waited 6 s while Idearium synced Nexus at boot (118 s, at 10–17% free memory); it waits 60 s, and every Idearium call that runs out of time now says which call, how long and that Idearium may be busy, never "signal is aborted". "Do the tests pass" for any project: the way is read from what the project holds — npm script, its own runner, Gradle, Maven, pytest, Cargo, Go, a Makefile test target — and a missing tool is said as tool_missing, not as failing tests (real Go and make runs in the test). test-any-project-tests 3/3, test-pipeline-root-causes 19/19, test-mastermind-ready 4/4. See CHANGELOG-0.60.201.md.] [0.60.200: James: "i just want you to add what idearium needs to work on mastermind." SD18, found by driving his MASTERMIND-v0_1_50 zip through Idearium's real API (import → index → roadmap → registry → graph → build) and fixing each stop: a project zipped in one folder (mm9/) lands at the repo root (stripWrapper on the project imports, said as wrapper); nine roadmap-named files are counted, the empty left out, the JSON-backed one taken (rankRoadmaps); the importer's evidence named phasemap/phases.json by path, so P1 was mapped to the roadmap itself — bare names now; the files PHASEMAP.md names under each phase are carried as named_in_source, so P1 gets its graph (schemas/node.schema.json, edge.schema.json) with no model; proof runs the project's own runner (node tests/run-all.js: 51 run, 3 skipped for electron) when there is no test script; integrity notes for 1,371 files fit (cut lines and bodies) and one failed note no longer stops the rest. The real run reaches the agent ladder for P1. test-mastermind-ready 4/4, test-roadmap-import 5/5. See CHANGELOG-0.60.200.md.] [0.60.199: James, uploading MASTERMIND-v0_1_50: "so i can import a project and have it continue? Like the one uploaded" · "yes." SD17: Idearium read a plan only from *-phasemap.spec, so a project that brought its own roadmap showed nothing to continue. idearium/repo/roadmap-import.js reads phases.json / roadmap.json / PHASEMAP.md / ROADMAP.md into a Nexus phasemap beside them: the project's ids (P3-UI → P3u, source_id kept), its states and percent, blocks and the markdown's critical-path chains as depends_on — each edge naming the file it came from, a line marked "unblocked" given none — exit criteria as proof; files left unknown for "map files & dependencies"; parsed back by loom before it is written, through the same writer as an added phase; an import is never replaced without replace. GET/POST /api/repos/:uuid/roadmaps[/import], `repo roadmaps`, `repo roadmap-import`, the Phases tab's offer (empty state and rail). MASTERMIND's real roadmap: 16 phases, 4 done, DAG clean, next P0 (9 phases wait on it). test-roadmap-import 5/5 (RI-05 in Clear Glass). The genesis fork stays in the tree, parked (not this release). See CHANGELOG-0.60.199.md.] [0.60.198: James: "yes, the registry should be the spine, event bus, interaction contract, and component registry." — his answer to whether the registry, not the atlas, is what a build requires. IN3a (docs/2026-10-19-infrastructure-spine-phasemap.spec): the snapshot's registryVersion is recorded as its four parts — components, spine (require wires), event bus (events with emitters and consumers), interaction contract (routes, CLI) — each hashed, read by the same idearium/repo/architecture.js the Architect tab and the planner use; a phase build requires it (index once, then NO_REGISTRY), and nothing in the build read atlas.json. The Versionium list shows the four counts. Fixed on the way: 0.60.197's reworded refusal broke test-mco3-versionium-tab (it reads "import pipeline"), restored. test-pipeline-root-causes 18/18 (RC-16 four parts on a real indexed repo, RC-18 the gate), mco3 39/39, mcob 30/30, versionium-tab 32/32, contract-phase-build 14/14. See CHANGELOG-0.60.198.md.] [0.60.197: James, of the atlas the build's snapshot required: "shouldn't that be the component registry. architecture tab". The registry the Architect tab shows (idearium/repo/architecture.js over the code-intel cards) is what the planner reads, and the snapshot did not record it. Every repo snapshot now records registryVersion: the sha256 of its components and wires, with counts; the Versionium list shows it. The atlas stays recorded (§0.3). Reading taken: record the registry as well; whether the registry should replace the atlas as what a build requires is his to confirm (both come from the same indexing today). test-mco3-repo-snapshot 39/39, test-mcob-snapshot-restore 30/30, test-pipeline-root-causes 17/17 (RC-16 asserts the registry). See CHANGELOG-0.60.197.md.] [0.60.196: James, with screenshots of the upload dialog left on "looking-glass-bundle-v0_4.zip — ready" and SP5 refused: "now its not working, i clicked the phases to build it and got: ... the import project ui needs to close when it's complete. "10:57 AMrefused no snapshot: repo has not been through the import pipeline (no atlas.json) — run POST /api/repos/:uuid/chunk first" what does it need an atlas for?" The snapshot before a build pins the atlas, chunk index and graph the build reads, so a repo with files and no index could not be snapshotted. The dialog's 30 s wait ran out while the server finished the import; it reset to "ready" and never started the indexing that follows. Now: a build that meets NOT_INDEXED runs the same import pipeline POST /chunk runs, writes an 'indexed' row on the run, and snapshots again; the dialog waits 120 s, and if the wait still runs out it finds the repo by the compartment the upload opened, closes, opens it and indexes; a real failure shows its reason instead of "ready". test-pipeline-root-causes 17/17 (RC-16 runs the pipeline on an unindexed repo and snapshots it; RC-17 is a source check of the dialog, not driven in Clear Glass). See CHANGELOG-0.60.196.md.] [0.60.195: James: "okay bvut did it not capture response from chatgtp." · "looking glaass is a test spec. rral spec test caser". No — ChatGPT's reply was not captured (0ch, no-reply-element; the /uc/ fix of 0.60.194 lets its transcript complete such a job now). The proposal "by chatgpt" was Gemini's: the work surface credited a run's last row, and the 'escalating' row names the next rung — each path now credits the agent whose reply brought it. Why nothing fixed SP5: Gemini delivered looking-glass-bundle-v0_4/lib/title.js and the shadow's exact match called lib/title.js absent, so every working attempt climbed the ladder and was discarded — a bundle's file under its own folder is now the file its map names (never a bare file name). Looking Glass recorded as his real test case for the pipeline. test-pipeline-root-causes 15/15 (RC-14, RC-15). See CHANGELOG-0.60.195.md.] [0.60.194: James: "its having so much issue with the title.js. nothing has fixed it. sp_5." · "why all of a sudden? with the clearglass?" From his log and screenshots: ChatGPT moved its chats to /uc/<id> and every reader keyed on /c/ — the userscript's chatId read 'home', so the chat ledger, the transcript reader and Clear Glass's chat ports dropped every ChatGPT chat (the "all of a sudden"); now /c/ or /uc/ (userscript 10.12.5). RC4b's repairTree ran only inside the test-first branch, so a resumed SP5 build kept "Gemini said tests/sp5-title.test.js" in the tree, blocking his Apply; every build now repairs after its snapshot (archived, never deleted). Said: ChatGPT's title.js never trims (' looking glass ' → ' Looking Glass ') — the regression gate was right to refuse it; Claude was signed out by claude.ai itself (no account-switch in the log). test-pipeline-root-causes 13/13 (RC-12, RC-13). See CHANGELOG-0.60.194.md.] [0.60.193: James, with a screenshot of NEXUS/CORE Home: "whats next". The screenshot showed "docs/atlases/nexus-atlas.md is binary" where the atlas should render — idearium/repo/nexus-self.js fileText() used the 200 KB chunk limit as a read limit and called anything over it binary (the atlas has been ~500 KB for weeks). A read-only read now allows 8 MB and says "too large" past it; chunking unchanged. test-nexus-atlas-and-glass AT-003 reads a 330 KB atlas (failed with his exact message before the fix). See CHANGELOG-0.60.193.md.] [0.60.192: James: "look in clearglass download manager". His 0.39.278 chat ledger (clear-glass/src/downloads/chat-ledger.js) already keeps every chat as the page's DOM mutator streams it — 0.60.191 had built a second copy in guardian's .response nodes instead (§8.6 broken). Removed as a true duplicate (both files byte-identical to before 0.60.191, said). Built in its place: chat-transcripts ledgerReplyFor reads his ledger through Clear Glass's route (lib/chat-ledger-client.js), matches the job's turn, only once the page stopped generating; the dispatcher asks it before a re-send and before failing a dead tab's job. Found: lib/work-journal.js read the ledger at :7701 (the SSE port) — every read outside the tests failed; one client on :7702 now. test-stream-fallback 4/4 against the real chat-ledger.js. See CHANGELOG-0.60.192.md.] [0.60.191: James: "each system will use its own component registry." · "im saying to use those as fallback routes. node anchor, dom mutator. I love the way idearium tabs are and way it shows me whats being said from the agents in real time." The stream as a fallback route (Guardian R1/I1, no new phase): the DOM mutator's chunks at the reply's node anchor — what Idearium's tabs show live — were broadcast and kept nowhere a recovery reads; now the job's .response node holds them as 'partial' (never over a complete one), the dom pulse's generating:false marks a quiet stream ended, and job-retry's answerFirst reads job → complete node → transcript → ended stream before it re-sends, and the dispatcher asks it before failing a typed job whose tab died (GS-04b). His registry decision recorded on the spine map (each system its own registry, one shape — DB9 already builds per system). test-stream-fallback 4/4 (it caught the first chunk being throttled away). See CHANGELOG-0.60.191.md.] [0.60.190: James: "make sure it's all interconnected, changes are logged and the diff snapshotted in versionium with a change log when approved. what about an api route or the pulse emission system". APV1–APV5 (docs/approval-chain.spec): an approved change's ChangeEntry (lib/change-entry.js — the phase, why, files ±, proposer, approver, the run and who started it) is written INTO the Versionium commit of its diff (message + provenance.changelog; APV0, decided by his axioms §7.6/§0.3: one record); a gate approval's entry rides on its system's sync commit, back in the queue if that fails; GET /api/repos/:uuid/changelog, `repo changelog`, the drawer's Versions view; run started, change approved/reverted, version committed and released emitted on the orchestrator's ledger through postDurable, the buffer drained every minute (it never was — failure modes too), counted on GET /api/ledger/out. test-approval-chain 6/6 against the real API and Versionium's own routes, APC-06 in Clear Glass. See CHANGELOG-0.60.190.md.] [0.60.189: James: "It built 5 phases in a row." RC7: every run names who started it — startedBy { who, whence } on every idearium_phase_runs row (access: the gate's keyed caller; declared: person:ui, person:cli, agent:<name>; system: watchdog:<kind>; unknown, never guessed), shown as "by …" on each run in the Plan panel and in `repo phase`. Traced: no code path builds a different phase unasked, but a listener runs up to 5 command lines per heard reply through the agent tool, and `repo phase <p> build` is not the person's alone — RC-11 drives five builds from one reply, each now recorded as agent:listener. Whether a listener may start builds stays his call. test-pipeline-root-causes 11/11, test-plan-pieces PP-02 in Clear Glass. See CHANGELOG-0.60.189.md.] [0.60.188: James: "It built 5 phases in a row. I think I see what you mean but there are way more idearium phases than that." · "go ahead with the census". CNS1 (decided by his axioms, BX0e): lib/phase-census.js judges every phase against its evidence — commits that name it and touched its code, release lines, its files, its proof's tests and ids — with `phases census` and GET /api/phases/census; docs/idearium-phase-census.md generated. 733 Idearium phases: done 330, partly 12, mentioned 11, not-built 206, no-files 92, deferred 82; 0 built-but-marked-open; 321 open bottom-up. Found and fixed at the cause: loom's phase parser silently skipped COSA1_, MR3b_, TB7a_ and BX0a-style ids and nested decisions (DAG 1900 → 1925 phases, dangling 25 → 17); his BX0b–d answers merged into the decisions block. test-phase-census 6/6. See CHANGELOG-0.60.188.md.] [0.60.187: James chose the order — "Fixes, then graph (Recommended)" — after asking to go "back to the pipeline workflow and automation in idearium". RC5 found: lib/repo-inject.js fromReply took the FIRST LINE of the agent's path answer as the path, and Gemini's page text starts with its screen-reader line "Gemini said" (so "Gemini said tests/sp5-title.test.js"); the cut names were answers read mid-stream (replyFor had no settled check). Fixed at the cause: path-guard stripSpeaker + pathAnswer (one complete path or none) in fromReply, the label stripped in chat-transcripts and in the Gemini userscript, replyFor waits for a settled transcript. RC6 found: the diagnostic service's gap escalation posted ownerless jobs to the Ollama bridge every 30 s sweep with the 4096-token default (num_ctx 5120 — his log's numbers), marked gaps through a private store copy cortex never saw, and forgot its cooldown on restart. Fixed: the bridge refuses ownerless jobs and logs the owner; diagnose is background work; the escalation names itself, asks for 1024 tokens, one per sweep, a 6 h cooldown on disk, status through cortex's new POST /api/gaps/status. Proof: test-pipeline-root-causes 10/10. Not run on his Windows machine.]
+  system: '0.60.224',   // [James, 2026-10-28: "continue" — and, earlier the same day, "like none of the autofill works" · "use it for correcting or creating macros/automation". Found the reason the Fiverr panel never did anything: it read window.cg, which nothing ever set (since 0.59.3), so it returned on its first line and none of its buttons were wired; the page-map panel the same. Both now get the wrapped API. And what the gig cannot hold (Service type, the metadata boxes, the pricing rows) he records once: "Record my choices on this page" keeps it as that page's macro, and every fill of the page replays it — a ticked box replays as ticked (not a toggle), the recording's own gig address is dropped, each click waits for its element, Save / Continue / Publish are left out by what the button said. browser_action names check, select, choose and pointer. test-fiverr-works 11/11 (FW-10 drives the real browser.html in Clear Glass, and fails without the fix); macro 22/22, live-recording 8/8, browser-shell 28/28. See CHANGELOG-0.60.224.md.] [James, 2026-10-28: "continue get it working" — two gaps that would still stop it on the live site, closed. His Edit Gig was already open in a bare pop-out window, and a page in a bare window has no tab strip, so its links still popped out: a link meant as a tab from any bare window now opens as a tab of the main window (sized sign-in popups stay windows). And choose found a dropdown's options only when they were list items or named option/item: options that are plain divs are now found by what appeared on screen when the dropdown opened. test-fiverr-works 9/9 (a plain-div dropdown chosen in Clear Glass); test-compartment-window back to its base result. See CHANGELOG-0.60.223.md.] [James, 2026-10-28: "like autofill should detect pages, so it can know when to paste, or detect the actual elements. like hook into the element picker, mayne use it for correcting or creating macros/automation" — said with screenshots of Fiverr's Overview and Scope & Pricing. The gig fill now reads the page first and knows which editor page it is on (from what the page shows: Overview, Scope & Pricing, or unknown, said), and does that page's parts only: on Overview it clicks the suggested category that matches the gig's ("Programming & Tech > AI Development"); on Scope & Pricing it types the names and descriptions and chooses each tier's delivery time. What it cannot find is listed in the gig panel with "Point at it": the element picker starts, the element he clicks becomes a selector that finds exactly it, kept for that part on that site and page, and used first on every fill after. The panel's count printed the list of filled fields, not a number; fixed. test-fiverr-works 9/9 (the pick → selector driven in Clear Glass). See CHANGELOG-0.60.222.md.] [James, 2026-10-28: "why did fiverr open in a popout window? like none of the autofill works." · "it needs to do the drop down menus. one by one" · "stop. just get fiverr working." Fiverr, made to work, three causes fixed, each from the code. Edit Gig is a target=_blank link, and Clear Glass opened every such link as a bare window that autofill, the driver and macros never reach; a link meant as a tab now opens as a tab of the window it is in (a sized sign-in popup stays a window). The pricing boxes have only placeholders, matched below the fill's floor, so they were never typed into; Fiverr's exact wording now counts. The dropdowns were skipped on purpose; ClearDriver's new choose opens one by column and row (or the text it shows) with the curved pointer, picks the option meant ('3' is 3 Days, never 30), clicks it and reads it back, and the gig fill chooses each tier's delivery time one by one. Driven in Clear Glass on a page laid out like Fiverr's Scope & Pricing: test-fiverr-works 5/5. See CHANGELOG-0.60.221.md.] [James, 2026-10-23: "Also need a processes screen for background tabs and activity." SP6, the screen: a Processes window — Shift+Esc as in Firefox and Chrome, ☰ → Processes, Tools → Processes — with every process and its pages (kind, site, memory, CPU, PID; sortable, the sort and selection kept across the 2 s refresh; Pages only), Close on a background tab and End on a site's tab, each confirmed and his alone, and the activity beside it filtered by kind and site; a banner when Clear Glass does not answer. This finishes his request of the day (SP1–SP6, 0.60.215–0.60.220). Driven in Clear Glass against the real backend: test-processes-ui 4/4; test-browser-shell 28/28 (BST-17 clicks the new ☰ row). See CHANGELOG-0.60.220.md.] [James, 2026-10-23: "Also need a processes screen for background tabs and activity." SP5, the backend of the Processes screen (the screen is SP6, next): clear-glass/src/processes lists every Clear Glass process with its pages — a site's tab, a background tab by name with its memory, a provider tab, a window — joined from Electron's own process metrics; ends one only for the person (a background tab is closed, a site's renderer ended, a Clear Glass window refused); and keeps the last 500 things that happened, read off the bus every system already uses (the driver's actions, background tabs, downloads, workflow runs, errors) plus page loads and macro runs. IPC, /cli/processes and /cli/activity (an agent can read, not end), commands glass processes, glass activity and site. Found on the way: an agent could lift a limit he set on it through /cli/site-profile or /cli/invoke — the keys that limit agents are now his alone. test-processes 4/4. See CHANGELOG-0.60.219.md.] [James, 2026-10-23: "I need site specific settings for the autofill, macros, workflows, passwords/accounts, snowflake, resume state. Cleardriver, agentmesh and guardian setting." SP4: the global settings ClearDriver, the agent mesh and Guardian lacked. Options driver (navigate 30 s, wait 10 s, agents may drive sites), mesh (2 tries per strategy; waits of 1, 3, 8 and 15 s before retry, respawn, fallback, diagnostic) and guardian (page listeners, chat capture) — each default today's constant, so nothing changes until he changes it; a site's own choice wins over them. New Settings sections ClearDriver and Guardian (with listener decay, which had no control since 0.39.265); the agent mesh gets "When a task fails"; the mesh reads its drain at each failure, a bad value not used and said. test-global-settings 5/5 (GS-05 in Clear Glass). See CHANGELOG-0.60.218.md.] [James, 2026-10-23: "I need site specific settings for the autofill, macros, workflows, passwords/accounts, snowflake, resume state. Cleardriver, agentmesh and guardian setting." SP3: the screens. The toolbar's Site Settings popup is now the tab's own site — nine groups (Autofill, Macros, Workflows, Passwords & account, Network, Resume, ClearDriver, Co-pilot & agent mesh, Guardian), every control drawn from the key table, each change written at once, each row marked "this site" with ↺, "Settings" or "default", a refused value's reason on its row; the Network row is the site's Snowflake route. Settings › Sites' Edit dialog has the same groups, written on Save. One builder for both (renderer/site-profile-groups.js). Driven in Clear Glass against the real resolver: test-site-settings-ui 3/3; test-browser-shell 28/28. See CHANGELOG-0.60.217.md.] [James, 2026-10-23: "I need site specific settings for the autofill, macros, workflows, passwords/accounts, snowflake, resume state. Cleardriver, agentmesh and guardian setting." SP2: each feature now reads its site value at the point it decides (clear-glass/src/site-settings/readers.js, installed by main over the one resolver). ClearDriver refuses an agent a site whose settings say agents may not drive it, and uses the site's navigate / wait timeouts; his own runs are never refused. Passwords: never offer, no autofill, and the account that signs in, per site. Autofill: off, or the site's profile. Workflows that fire on a page visit are skipped where off; a site's macros run when its pages load, as his own run. Co-pilot answers with the site's agent, or without the page where the mesh is off. Guardian: no listeners, or no chat capture, where off. Resume state: the page keeps its place as he leaves and gets it back on a revisit, scroll and forms, never a password (src/rewind/resume.js) — rewind's snapshots could not, they are taken at load on agent navigations; said in the spec's addendum. Unset sites behave as before. test-site-readers 7/7. See CHANGELOG-0.60.216.md.] [James, 2026-10-23: "I need site specific settings for the autofill, macros, workflows, passwords/accounts, snowflake, resume state. Cleardriver, agentmesh and guardian setting. Also need a processes screen for background tabs and activity." SP1 of docs/2026-10-23-site-profile-processes-phasemap.spec: one table of per-site keys for every feature he named (clear-glass/src/site-settings/keys.js, defaults are today's behaviour) and one resolver, siteProfile(url) — the site's value, else the global option, else the default, each marked with where it came from — over the per-origin store Clear Glass already had; the Snowflake route per site is one netPolicy origin rule (site.<host>), checked on a copy first so a refused route keeps the one it had. IPC site-profile:*, /cli/site-profile, cg.siteProfile. No feature reads it yet (SP2), and there is no screen yet (SP3). Readings to confirm: "resume state" read as where he was on a site on a revisit; "snowflake" per site read as the site's route. test-site-profile 3/3. See CHANGELOG-0.60.215.md.] [James, with a screenshot of the ☰ menu's Tools: "also still here? why? supposed to be migrated". Agent Mesh, Element Picker and NCP Providers moved into Guardian's agents hub in 0.60.180 (shell/agents.js); 0.60.210's rebuilt Tools listed every unpinned tool and brought them back — the coder's miss. The ☰ menu now leaves the hub's tools to the hub; Customize toolbar can still pin them and says "In the agents hub". Fiverr gigs, DOM Archaeology, Rewind session and Toggle Co-pilot are not in the hub and stay. Also: a ☰ open that missed once in a run (BST-07, BST-17, BST-23) now reports the page's errors and the panel's state; six full runs after it were 28/28, so its cause is not yet caught — said, not claimed fixed. See CHANGELOG-0.60.214.md.] [James, with a screenshot of the start page: "such a small thing but get rid of that border in the search. comit but also hand me the file you change." The shell's keyboard-focus ring (body.cg-shell :focus-visible, shell.css) outranked the search input's outline: 0 and drew a second box inside the field; the field's own glow already shows focus, so the input now draws none (renderer/shell/shell.css). Checked in Clear Glass: BST-22 asserts no ring on the focused input and the field's glow kept; test-browser-shell 28/28. See CHANGELOG-0.60.213.md.] [James: "then resume data migratino" · "Architect 1 isn't a system i don't believe and can be deleted." DL3: loom, copilot and intelligence hold their own files (cortex-sync's buffer, loom's uploads and scaffolds, copilot's diagnose output, intelligence's DATA_DIR and synthesis); no code names their root paths, Diagnostic's table points each at <folder>/data, and nexus-status's LoomDriver no longer reads the root data/loom shadow. Ten systems rewired; left: idearium, cortex, the shared data/ledger/. Architect was read before acting: a running system (port 3747, 14 routes, Idearium's compiler bridge calls its map scan), so its removal is put to him with that cost, not done. The 27 tests that load these modules match 0.60.211 apart from test-guardian-own-data, whose example of a system not yet rewired was loom — it now picks one. See CHANGELOG-0.60.212.md.] [James, 2026-10-22: "can you integrate that into clearglass, its a basic funcaiton of any browser." · "why remove the agents from new tab?" Zoom has been in Clear Glass's core since 0.60.183; what it lacked was a place on the toolbar — Zoom controls (− level +, the level resets to 100%) is now a pinnable built-in tool on the same core zoom as Ctrl +/−, the address bar's level and the ☰ menu's Zoom row. (0.60.210's zoom-plugin note came from the test fixture, not his install.) The start page's agents were not removed: the page read the list once and a failed early read stayed as "Guardian's provider host is not answering" — it now names what failed and reads the agents again every 3 s while open. test-browser-shell 28/28 (BST-22, BST-23 in Clear Glass). See CHANGELOG-0.60.211.md.] [James, 2026-10-22, with a screenshot of the ☰ menu: "customize toolbar and those toolbar links, and customize toolbar need to be rebuilt for  the new ui, they are cheap and crappy, and look bad." The tool rows and the pinned tool buttons drew text glyphs beside drawn icons. Each command now has a drawn icon and a line on what it does (src/toolbar/commands.js); the ☰ menu's Tools are the menu's own rows with a pin button and an Edit link; Customize toolbar is rebuilt on the shell's system — a live preview, two lists with each tool's icon and description, Pin / Unpin on every row, run-once actions as buttons. Found on the way: a plugin command with no button of its own was offered for pinning, which did nothing — it runs once now; Settings was listed twice. test-browser-shell 26/26 (BST-21 in Clear Glass). Also merged to main: 0.60.206–0.60.209 (HorrisEllis/Nexus#143). See CHANGELOG-0.60.210.md.] [0.60.209: James: "continue" — after Guardian, the next systems hold their own data. DL3 for six owners: versionium, ollama-bridge, architect, clear-glass, diagnostic and orchestrator resolve their data through lib/data-home.js; no code names their root data/ paths (test-data-rewired RW-01), Diagnostic's table — the registry's dataDir — points each at <folder>/data (RW-02), and at each one's boot its files come home (versionium 8, ollama 2, architect 1, clear-glass 1, diagnostic 5, orchestrator 5 in this tree). Their tables in cortex's store (SS2) follow per system; the order is corrected in the map's addendum. Found by running the tests one by one: ollama-command-index started ollama/server.js outside the sandbox, and its boot moved this checkout's real data/ollama files — the test now takes the sandbox, and data-home itself now sandboxes any test process before computing a path (as every resolver does); the clear-up's rollback rightly refused files the running server had appended to. The 68 tests that load these modules match 0.60.208 but one, test-agent-facts, whose check is now its intent (the cache never in the repo). See CHANGELOG-0.60.209.md.] [0.60.208: James: "start the data migration. look at the phases, any migrating data to the respective systems, and make each system responsible for it's own data." SS1, the first system end to end: Guardian. Its three requires of cortex's store are gone (data blockers 3 → 0): the callto agent keeps agent_calls, guardian_health, artifacts and toasts in Guardian's own store (agent_calls brought from cortex once, recorded) and sends event_log and cortex_memory through cortex's routes; bridge turns, flap events and flapping gaps go through cortex's POST /api/event and /api/gaps (buffered when cortex is down), and cortex keeps queue_uuid/comp_id top-level so the gap engine still matches them. DL3 for Guardian: every path resolves through lib/data-home.js — no code names data/guardian, so its files come home to guardian/data at its next boot; ledger-writer and cortex-write put a rewired system in its own folder and leave the rest. Found: guardian-job-persistence wrote real job files into the tree; it now asks the resolver (the sandbox). Not yet: data/ledger/guardian waits with every system's channel ledgers (15 code files name data/ledger). test-guardian-own-data 5/5; the 84 tests that load these modules match 0.60.207 except four that pinned the old path, updated. See CHANGELOG-0.60.208.md.] [0.60.207: James: "start the data migration. look at the phases, any migrating data to the respective systems, and make each system responsible for it's own data." The phases: docs/2026-10-21-data-layout-phasemap.spec (DL0, DL1 done) with the system-store track (SS1–SS4). DL2 built: lib/data-home.js resolves each system's data under <system>/data (home, ledger, nodes, memory; the sandbox in a test), and each system's entry now calls atBoot first — its files in the root data/ come home through the clear-up tool (manifest, sha256, rollback), a file whose old path code still names waits and is listed, his calls never move, and a boot with the same release reads nothing. Route /api/data/home, `data home`, `data rollback`, Settings console → Data. Today most files wait: the census finds code still naming every owner's root path. Next: SS1 (Guardian end to end), then each owner's files and tables together. test-data-home 6/6 (DH-06 in Clear Glass). See CHANGELOG-0.60.207.md.] [0.60.206: James, 2026-10-22, with five screenshots of SP5: "im so sick of this phase, been stuck on 2 days. the file is in  the directory already for the phase, so what is broken?" What was broken: Looking Glass was imported before 0.60.200 stripped a zip's wrapper folder, so the whole project sits under looking-glass-bundle-v0_4/ while SP5 names lib/title.js at the root; the build, the proof and the agent all look at the root, and each attempt wrote a second lib/title.js there. SD22: idearium/repo/lift-root.js lifts a project out of its one wrapper folder (most files, a project marker at its top, none at the root) through a new repo-layer moveFile; a root file at a path the project needs (the agents' copies) is archived under .nexus/archive/lift-root/ first, never deleted. GET/POST /api/repos/:uuid/lift-root, `repo lift-root <repo> [--apply]`, the offer in the Phases tab and in Environment. test-lift-root 3/3. From the same screenshots, "the agent button does nothing in the code tab.. aloso the ui for clearglass is cut off.": the Code tab's agent button showed on the changes view, which never drew the panel, and a picked line held the panel open on files — the panel now sits beside the changes and a press always shows or hides it (test-code-tab CT-11b, Clear Glass); a closed Co-pilot kept its 340 px because the 0.60.180 shell rule outranked .hidden — the black strip down the right of every page (test-browser-shell BST-09b at 1920 px: 340 before, 0 after). See CHANGELOG-0.60.206.md.] [0.60.205: James, with his 0.60.201 run log: "show me idearium improvements that would help the most from the phases" · "the log fixes also. start them". SD21, six fixes read from the log: the background probe asks whether its model fits free memory before loading it (its own 7b load drove memory critical every ten minutes); a build's job behind a sign-in wall is sent back so the ladder climbs (claude held one 295 s), a person's still waits; the shadow's absent line says file / event / condition (an unmet condition read like a missing file — the coder first misread it too); a proof run's per-run history stays out of the repo's store (35 → 65 spec chunks in an evening); a phase that asked for one file answers the "which file?" question itself; the Nexus sync pauses between systems when memory goes. test-log-fixes 6/6, test-proof-run 7/7. Not yet: the signed-out claude tab reconnecting every ~9 s. See CHANGELOG-0.60.205.md.] [0.60.204: James: "also fix chatgpt. i can't login. i click login and it pops up for a second and dissapears". "Continue with Google" opens a popup that takes focus; the ChatGPT window behind it was visible but unfocused, which the attention handler read as the hidden agent tab, so it sent ChatGPT (works signed out) back to its chat and the popup, its opener gone, closed. A window with its own sign-in popup open is now his: not sent back, not focused, not hidden mid-sign-in (clear-glass/src/providers/attention.js 1.4.0; login.live.com, auth0.openai.com and Cloudflare's challenge count as sign-in pages). test-cg-attention-focus 7/7 (AF-02c). See CHANGELOG-0.60.204.md.] [0.60.203: James, after his SP5 run at 1–9% free memory, to the coder's three fixes (stop the officiator's loop; a pinned local model that won't fit goes to the browser agents and says why; a build step never auto-applies a file it wasn't asked for): "yes". SD20. (1) The officiator turned each of the build's replies into the same goal ("Build phase SP5_title by ensuring lib/title.js…") about every 30 s, each contract blocked or failed: a goal already queued, running or failed within the hour is now marked on the drop (duplicateOf), not queued again; after 3 of its own contracts fail in a row it stops synthesizing for an hour (unpark all clears it); memory is read before each synthesis, and the RAID worker waits on memory too. (2) Settings pinning Ollama with no ladder now climbs the pin, then the chain's agents: a model that is missing or won't fit free memory is left off, critical memory moves it last, said on the route. (3) While a phase builds, an agent's file outside the phase's files — or a spec/doc while it asked for code — is held as a proposal with why, whatever the inject mode (repo-inject land and code-edit commit). test-starved-machine 5/5, test-raid-officiator 18/18. See CHANGELOG-0.60.203.md.] [0.60.202: James, after SP5's title.js was refused again and again: "need a retry or automatically ask for the fix if possible, or a tool or something" · "still didn't work." · "that error needs to be fixed." The gate refused each Gemini attempt because it changed the error message tests/sp5-title.test.js checks, and Gemini never saw which test or what it expected. SD19: an agent's refused proposal now goes back to its agent the way his Reject does — rejected with the failing test, expected and actual, as the reason; told; the phase built again within the daily cap — and the answer says so (repos.retry_on_refusal:false leaves it to him; a person's change is untouched). Toasts: one identical message counted ×N instead of five stacked boxes; a long one shows its first line, the whole on hover. test-refusal-sendback 4/4 (RS-03 in Clear Glass). See CHANGELOG-0.60.202.md.] [0.60.201: James, with his upload dialog showing "could not start: signal is aborted without reason" and his boot log: "okay, remember this is for any project. not just mmine". The dialog's first step waited 6 s while Idearium synced Nexus at boot (118 s, at 10–17% free memory); it waits 60 s, and every Idearium call that runs out of time now says which call, how long and that Idearium may be busy, never "signal is aborted". "Do the tests pass" for any project: the way is read from what the project holds — npm script, its own runner, Gradle, Maven, pytest, Cargo, Go, a Makefile test target — and a missing tool is said as tool_missing, not as failing tests (real Go and make runs in the test). test-any-project-tests 3/3, test-pipeline-root-causes 19/19, test-mastermind-ready 4/4. See CHANGELOG-0.60.201.md.] [0.60.200: James: "i just want you to add what idearium needs to work on mastermind." SD18, found by driving his MASTERMIND-v0_1_50 zip through Idearium's real API (import → index → roadmap → registry → graph → build) and fixing each stop: a project zipped in one folder (mm9/) lands at the repo root (stripWrapper on the project imports, said as wrapper); nine roadmap-named files are counted, the empty left out, the JSON-backed one taken (rankRoadmaps); the importer's evidence named phasemap/phases.json by path, so P1 was mapped to the roadmap itself — bare names now; the files PHASEMAP.md names under each phase are carried as named_in_source, so P1 gets its graph (schemas/node.schema.json, edge.schema.json) with no model; proof runs the project's own runner (node tests/run-all.js: 51 run, 3 skipped for electron) when there is no test script; integrity notes for 1,371 files fit (cut lines and bodies) and one failed note no longer stops the rest. The real run reaches the agent ladder for P1. test-mastermind-ready 4/4, test-roadmap-import 5/5. See CHANGELOG-0.60.200.md.] [0.60.199: James, uploading MASTERMIND-v0_1_50: "so i can import a project and have it continue? Like the one uploaded" · "yes." SD17: Idearium read a plan only from *-phasemap.spec, so a project that brought its own roadmap showed nothing to continue. idearium/repo/roadmap-import.js reads phases.json / roadmap.json / PHASEMAP.md / ROADMAP.md into a Nexus phasemap beside them: the project's ids (P3-UI → P3u, source_id kept), its states and percent, blocks and the markdown's critical-path chains as depends_on — each edge naming the file it came from, a line marked "unblocked" given none — exit criteria as proof; files left unknown for "map files & dependencies"; parsed back by loom before it is written, through the same writer as an added phase; an import is never replaced without replace. GET/POST /api/repos/:uuid/roadmaps[/import], `repo roadmaps`, `repo roadmap-import`, the Phases tab's offer (empty state and rail). MASTERMIND's real roadmap: 16 phases, 4 done, DAG clean, next P0 (9 phases wait on it). test-roadmap-import 5/5 (RI-05 in Clear Glass). The genesis fork stays in the tree, parked (not this release). See CHANGELOG-0.60.199.md.] [0.60.198: James: "yes, the registry should be the spine, event bus, interaction contract, and component registry." — his answer to whether the registry, not the atlas, is what a build requires. IN3a (docs/2026-10-19-infrastructure-spine-phasemap.spec): the snapshot's registryVersion is recorded as its four parts — components, spine (require wires), event bus (events with emitters and consumers), interaction contract (routes, CLI) — each hashed, read by the same idearium/repo/architecture.js the Architect tab and the planner use; a phase build requires it (index once, then NO_REGISTRY), and nothing in the build read atlas.json. The Versionium list shows the four counts. Fixed on the way: 0.60.197's reworded refusal broke test-mco3-versionium-tab (it reads "import pipeline"), restored. test-pipeline-root-causes 18/18 (RC-16 four parts on a real indexed repo, RC-18 the gate), mco3 39/39, mcob 30/30, versionium-tab 32/32, contract-phase-build 14/14. See CHANGELOG-0.60.198.md.] [0.60.197: James, of the atlas the build's snapshot required: "shouldn't that be the component registry. architecture tab". The registry the Architect tab shows (idearium/repo/architecture.js over the code-intel cards) is what the planner reads, and the snapshot did not record it. Every repo snapshot now records registryVersion: the sha256 of its components and wires, with counts; the Versionium list shows it. The atlas stays recorded (§0.3). Reading taken: record the registry as well; whether the registry should replace the atlas as what a build requires is his to confirm (both come from the same indexing today). test-mco3-repo-snapshot 39/39, test-mcob-snapshot-restore 30/30, test-pipeline-root-causes 17/17 (RC-16 asserts the registry). See CHANGELOG-0.60.197.md.] [0.60.196: James, with screenshots of the upload dialog left on "looking-glass-bundle-v0_4.zip — ready" and SP5 refused: "now its not working, i clicked the phases to build it and got: ... the import project ui needs to close when it's complete. "10:57 AMrefused no snapshot: repo has not been through the import pipeline (no atlas.json) — run POST /api/repos/:uuid/chunk first" what does it need an atlas for?" The snapshot before a build pins the atlas, chunk index and graph the build reads, so a repo with files and no index could not be snapshotted. The dialog's 30 s wait ran out while the server finished the import; it reset to "ready" and never started the indexing that follows. Now: a build that meets NOT_INDEXED runs the same import pipeline POST /chunk runs, writes an 'indexed' row on the run, and snapshots again; the dialog waits 120 s, and if the wait still runs out it finds the repo by the compartment the upload opened, closes, opens it and indexes; a real failure shows its reason instead of "ready". test-pipeline-root-causes 17/17 (RC-16 runs the pipeline on an unindexed repo and snapshots it; RC-17 is a source check of the dialog, not driven in Clear Glass). See CHANGELOG-0.60.196.md.] [0.60.195: James: "okay bvut did it not capture response from chatgtp." · "looking glaass is a test spec. rral spec test caser". No — ChatGPT's reply was not captured (0ch, no-reply-element; the /uc/ fix of 0.60.194 lets its transcript complete such a job now). The proposal "by chatgpt" was Gemini's: the work surface credited a run's last row, and the 'escalating' row names the next rung — each path now credits the agent whose reply brought it. Why nothing fixed SP5: Gemini delivered looking-glass-bundle-v0_4/lib/title.js and the shadow's exact match called lib/title.js absent, so every working attempt climbed the ladder and was discarded — a bundle's file under its own folder is now the file its map names (never a bare file name). Looking Glass recorded as his real test case for the pipeline. test-pipeline-root-causes 15/15 (RC-14, RC-15). See CHANGELOG-0.60.195.md.] [0.60.194: James: "its having so much issue with the title.js. nothing has fixed it. sp_5." · "why all of a sudden? with the clearglass?" From his log and screenshots: ChatGPT moved its chats to /uc/<id> and every reader keyed on /c/ — the userscript's chatId read 'home', so the chat ledger, the transcript reader and Clear Glass's chat ports dropped every ChatGPT chat (the "all of a sudden"); now /c/ or /uc/ (userscript 10.12.5). RC4b's repairTree ran only inside the test-first branch, so a resumed SP5 build kept "Gemini said tests/sp5-title.test.js" in the tree, blocking his Apply; every build now repairs after its snapshot (archived, never deleted). Said: ChatGPT's title.js never trims (' looking glass ' → ' Looking Glass ') — the regression gate was right to refuse it; Claude was signed out by claude.ai itself (no account-switch in the log). test-pipeline-root-causes 13/13 (RC-12, RC-13). See CHANGELOG-0.60.194.md.] [0.60.193: James, with a screenshot of NEXUS/CORE Home: "whats next". The screenshot showed "docs/atlases/nexus-atlas.md is binary" where the atlas should render — idearium/repo/nexus-self.js fileText() used the 200 KB chunk limit as a read limit and called anything over it binary (the atlas has been ~500 KB for weeks). A read-only read now allows 8 MB and says "too large" past it; chunking unchanged. test-nexus-atlas-and-glass AT-003 reads a 330 KB atlas (failed with his exact message before the fix). See CHANGELOG-0.60.193.md.] [0.60.192: James: "look in clearglass download manager". His 0.39.278 chat ledger (clear-glass/src/downloads/chat-ledger.js) already keeps every chat as the page's DOM mutator streams it — 0.60.191 had built a second copy in guardian's .response nodes instead (§8.6 broken). Removed as a true duplicate (both files byte-identical to before 0.60.191, said). Built in its place: chat-transcripts ledgerReplyFor reads his ledger through Clear Glass's route (lib/chat-ledger-client.js), matches the job's turn, only once the page stopped generating; the dispatcher asks it before a re-send and before failing a dead tab's job. Found: lib/work-journal.js read the ledger at :7701 (the SSE port) — every read outside the tests failed; one client on :7702 now. test-stream-fallback 4/4 against the real chat-ledger.js. See CHANGELOG-0.60.192.md.] [0.60.191: James: "each system will use its own component registry." · "im saying to use those as fallback routes. node anchor, dom mutator. I love the way idearium tabs are and way it shows me whats being said from the agents in real time." The stream as a fallback route (Guardian R1/I1, no new phase): the DOM mutator's chunks at the reply's node anchor — what Idearium's tabs show live — were broadcast and kept nowhere a recovery reads; now the job's .response node holds them as 'partial' (never over a complete one), the dom pulse's generating:false marks a quiet stream ended, and job-retry's answerFirst reads job → complete node → transcript → ended stream before it re-sends, and the dispatcher asks it before failing a typed job whose tab died (GS-04b). His registry decision recorded on the spine map (each system its own registry, one shape — DB9 already builds per system). test-stream-fallback 4/4 (it caught the first chunk being throttled away). See CHANGELOG-0.60.191.md.] [0.60.190: James: "make sure it's all interconnected, changes are logged and the diff snapshotted in versionium with a change log when approved. what about an api route or the pulse emission system". APV1–APV5 (docs/approval-chain.spec): an approved change's ChangeEntry (lib/change-entry.js — the phase, why, files ±, proposer, approver, the run and who started it) is written INTO the Versionium commit of its diff (message + provenance.changelog; APV0, decided by his axioms §7.6/§0.3: one record); a gate approval's entry rides on its system's sync commit, back in the queue if that fails; GET /api/repos/:uuid/changelog, `repo changelog`, the drawer's Versions view; run started, change approved/reverted, version committed and released emitted on the orchestrator's ledger through postDurable, the buffer drained every minute (it never was — failure modes too), counted on GET /api/ledger/out. test-approval-chain 6/6 against the real API and Versionium's own routes, APC-06 in Clear Glass. See CHANGELOG-0.60.190.md.] [0.60.189: James: "It built 5 phases in a row." RC7: every run names who started it — startedBy { who, whence } on every idearium_phase_runs row (access: the gate's keyed caller; declared: person:ui, person:cli, agent:<name>; system: watchdog:<kind>; unknown, never guessed), shown as "by …" on each run in the Plan panel and in `repo phase`. Traced: no code path builds a different phase unasked, but a listener runs up to 5 command lines per heard reply through the agent tool, and `repo phase <p> build` is not the person's alone — RC-11 drives five builds from one reply, each now recorded as agent:listener. Whether a listener may start builds stays his call. test-pipeline-root-causes 11/11, test-plan-pieces PP-02 in Clear Glass. See CHANGELOG-0.60.189.md.] [0.60.188: James: "It built 5 phases in a row. I think I see what you mean but there are way more idearium phases than that." · "go ahead with the census". CNS1 (decided by his axioms, BX0e): lib/phase-census.js judges every phase against its evidence — commits that name it and touched its code, release lines, its files, its proof's tests and ids — with `phases census` and GET /api/phases/census; docs/idearium-phase-census.md generated. 733 Idearium phases: done 330, partly 12, mentioned 11, not-built 206, no-files 92, deferred 82; 0 built-but-marked-open; 321 open bottom-up. Found and fixed at the cause: loom's phase parser silently skipped COSA1_, MR3b_, TB7a_ and BX0a-style ids and nested decisions (DAG 1900 → 1925 phases, dangling 25 → 17); his BX0b–d answers merged into the decisions block. test-phase-census 6/6. See CHANGELOG-0.60.188.md.] [0.60.187: James chose the order — "Fixes, then graph (Recommended)" — after asking to go "back to the pipeline workflow and automation in idearium". RC5 found: lib/repo-inject.js fromReply took the FIRST LINE of the agent's path answer as the path, and Gemini's page text starts with its screen-reader line "Gemini said" (so "Gemini said tests/sp5-title.test.js"); the cut names were answers read mid-stream (replyFor had no settled check). Fixed at the cause: path-guard stripSpeaker + pathAnswer (one complete path or none) in fromReply, the label stripped in chat-transcripts and in the Gemini userscript, replyFor waits for a settled transcript. RC6 found: the diagnostic service's gap escalation posted ownerless jobs to the Ollama bridge every 30 s sweep with the 4096-token default (num_ctx 5120 — his log's numbers), marked gaps through a private store copy cortex never saw, and forgot its cooldown on restart. Fixed: the bridge refuses ownerless jobs and logs the owner; diagnose is background work; the escalation names itself, asks for